Spawn, objective 209 [observability] (the lead's follow-up order on 192 R4, 2026-11-17; rev-f's R4 n1; author rev-e): a guardrail stretch judged false ends only on a fresh reading with its figure in range, the same in a loop that keeps running as in one restarted.

Base: live main (192's code is there: task 435, main 20d6b1ba). after: [192].

Read first:
- crates/spawn-cli/src/alerts/guardrails.rs:
  - the module doc's judged-stretch paragraph (192 (5));
  - seed_judged;
  - guardrail_events (its out branch and its else branch);
  - out_since_of;
  - seeding;
  - the tests a_judged_stretch_still_out_after_a_restart_is_not_raised_again, a_judged_stretch_survives_a_first_reading_with_no_fresh_figure and a_taken_judged_stretch_ends_like_any_other;
- crates/spawn-cli/src/alerts.rs: judged_record (an `alert.judged` record the loop reads while it runs);
- rsi/scenarios/192.sh (h1)-(h4).

The problem. One judgement of a guardrail alert gives a second alert or not, depending on whether the loop restarted in between.
- A restarted loop keeps the judged out-since (guardrail_judged, from the alert rows it starts with) through readings with no range, no value or a stale figure. A fresh reading out takes it. After that take, it lives as any other count, so a reading with no range ends the stretch, and the next reading out is raised after its hold (192 R4's rule (a)).
- A loop that keeps running when the owner judges its alert only marks the row cleared in its view. A reading with no range drops guardrail_out, and the next reading out starts a new stretch, raised after its hold (rev-g's probe on 192 R3: it fires at t1 + 2 holds).

What changes (crates/spawn-cli/src/alerts.rs and alerts/guardrails.rs; no store change, no new read):
(1) An `alert.judged` record (a verdict other than "right") for an open guardrail_out_of_range alert the loop knows keeps that alert's figure and out-since (evidence out_since_ms) as a judged stretch. This is exactly what a restarted loop seeds from the alert rows (seed_judged). The figure is figure_of(subject, evidence). There is one exception (rev-i's F1 on R1): if this loop already made a fresh reading with the figure in range after that out-since, keep nothing. The alert was still open in its clear hold, but its stretch had ended, as a restarted loop's seeding ends it on a sample in range. The loop keeps each figure's newest reading in range (guardrail_in) for this.
(2) A judged stretch is kept until positive evidence ends it:
  - a fresh reading with the figure in range (state "in");
  - or, at a restarted loop's first reading, a sample in range after its out-since (seeding; unchanged).
  Every fresh reading out takes the judged out-since again: it is not consumed by the first take. So no range, not_collected and a stale figure end nothing, before or after a reading out. This replaces 192 R4's rule (a) for a taken stretch.
(3) With the judged out-since, the key is known, so no reading out raises it again however long the figure stays out. Once ended, the next stretch out is new: from now, or from the samples at a restarted loop's first reading, raised after its hold.

What stays as it is:
- A stretch not judged (089's clear hold):
  - an open alert's no_range or not_collected reading counts toward its clear hold;
  - an alert cleared by its condition is not a judged stretch, so a reading not out ends its count, and out again is a new alert;
  - the "gone" clear.
- 092: a judged alert is not cleared by the loop.
- The seeding rules of 192 (1)-(4).
- The alert's key, text and evidence.
- Judged entries are kept in memory per figure, at most one per figure (as 192: guardrail_judged).

Files:
- crates/spawn-cli/src/alerts.rs: judged_record, a few lines, and a guardrail_in field (each figure's newest reading in range, in memory, one entry per figure);
- crates/spawn-cli/src/alerts/guardrails.rs: a judged_stretch helper, out_since_of, the module doc, and unit tests:
  - a_taken_judged_stretch_ends_like_any_other is renamed a_taken_judged_stretch_ends_only_on_a_reading_in_range, with its assertion flipped;
  - new: an_alert_judged_while_the_loop_runs_stays_judged_through_no_range, which includes rev-i's probe (judged after a reading in range: the next stretch is raised).
Owner side:
- rsi/scenarios/209.sh (new);
- rsi/scenarios/192.sh: (h4) flipped, header and comment;
- rsi/scenarios/retired-tests: the renamed test;
- rsi/backlog/209.json.

Acceptance: rsi/scenarios/209.sh, black box through GET /api/alerts. It uses a real `spawn loop` and `spawn serve` on a scratch database, set up as 192.sh: leaked test schemas, its range from a ranges file (out, in, or none: no_range); no samples, the raise hold 15 s, readings every second, the clear hold an hour.
- (A) Continuous: an alert fires a hold after the start and is judged false while the loop runs. Then no range, then out for more than two holds: no new alert.
- (B) The same loop: in range, then out: a new alert, out since after the switch.
- (C) Restarted: (B)'s alert is judged false. A new loop is out at its first reading, then no range, then out for more than two holds: no new alert.
- (D) A stretch not judged: in range, then out: its alert fires. A new loop with the clear hold 2 s and no range clears it by its condition. Out again: a new alert after its hold.
- (E) (D)'s alert stays open in its clear hold (an hour again) while the figure reads in range, then no range. It is judged false, then out: a new alert after its hold. This passes on main too and guards (1)'s exception. No reading in range comes between the judgement and the switch, so only (1)'s exception can end the stretch.
- (F) (E)'s new alert is judged "right" while the loop runs, then has no range (the clear hold 2 s): it clears by its condition. Then out: a new alert after its hold. A "right" judgement keeps nothing judged.
On current main, (A) and (C) fail: each fires a new alert a hold after the reading out. 192.sh passes with (h4) flipped (on main its (h4) now fails the same way). Kept scenarios 089, 092, 176, 182 and 192 pass.
